          %h2#prequantum_geometry
            %strong Prequantum geometry
          %p
            There are two dual kinds of mathematical formalization of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantization"} quantization
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/deformation+quantization"} deformation quantization
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+quantization"} geometric quantization
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prequantization"} prequantization
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+geometry"} symplectic geometry
            obtained by lifting closed
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+2-forms"} differential 2-forms
            to being
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/curvature"} curvature
            forms of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/circle+bundles+with+connection"} circle bundles with connection
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/circle+bundles"} circle bundles
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/circle+bundles+with+connection"} circle bundles with connection
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+forms"} differential forms
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/automorphisms"} automorphisms
            (they form a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groupoid"} groupoid
            not just a
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetries"} symmetries
            of a prequantized symplectic manifold differs from the group of symmetries of the underlying symplectic manifold (the
            = succeed "):" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectomorphisms"} symplectomorphisms
            = succeed "-bundle" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U(1)
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/central+extension"} central extension
            = succeed "-central" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U(1)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern+class"} Chern class
            with coefficients in
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℤ
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z} = \pi_1(U(1))
            = succeed "\u201D" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantomorphism"} quantomorphism
            = succeed "-extensions" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U(1)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+algebra"} Lie algebra
            subsumes the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Heisenberg+algebra"} Heisenberg algebra
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+vector+spaces"} symplectic vector spaces
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a{:href => "#TheGeometricIdeaOfQuantization"} The geometric idea of quantization
          %p
            Hence it makes good sense to study prequantized smooth manifolds as geometries in their own right, and a sensible name for this is
            = succeed "." do
              %em prequantum geometry
          %p
            As one passes from
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanics"} quantum mechanics
            to
            = succeed "-dimensional" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi p
                    %mo +
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (p+1)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
            such as to preserve the locality of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+quantum+field+theory"} local quantum field theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantization"} quantization
            involves not just
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+manifolds"} symplectic manifolds
            equipped with symplectic differential 2-forms, but involves “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/covariant+phase+space"}> symplectic currents
            ” which are
            = succeed "-forms." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi p
                    %mo +
                    %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (p+2)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/de+Donder-Weyl-Hamilton+equation"} de Donder-Weyl-Hamilton equation
            of local
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+field+theory"} classical field theory
            and approaches to promote their theory to a higher analog of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+geometry"} symplectic geometry
            go by the names
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/multisymplectic+geometry"} multisymplectic geometry
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-plectic+geometry"} (p+1)-plectic geometry
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+prequantization"} higher prequantization
            of such a higher degree form is naturally a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/circle+n-bundle+with+connection"} circle (p+1)-bundle with connection
            (a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bundle+gerbe+with+connection"} bundle gerbe with connection
            for
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                    %mo =
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p = 1
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cocycle"} cocycle
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Deligne+cohomology"} Deligne cohomology
            of degree
            = succeed ")," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                    %mo +
                    %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p+2
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+prequantum+geometry"} higher prequantum geometry
            is the study of spaces that are equipped with such abelian
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principal+infinity-connections"} principal infinity-connections
          %p
            This is what we discuss here. More abstractly, if we we write
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi B
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝔾
                    %mi conn
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{B}\mathbb{G}_{conn}
            for the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+moduli+stack"} higher moduli stack
            of
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝔾
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{G}
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principal+infinity-connections"} principal infinity-connections
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi B
                    %mrow
                      %mi p
                      %mo +
                      %mn 1
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi conn
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{B}^{p+1}U(1)_{conn}
            the moduli stack for cocycles in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Deligne+cohomology"} Deligne cohomology
            in degree
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi p
                    %mo +
                    %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (p+2)
            = succeed "-bundle" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝔾
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{G}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+homotopy+type"} smooth homotopy type
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is equivalently just a morphism
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ∇
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi X
                    %mo ⟶
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi B
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝔾
                      %mi conn
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \nabla \colon X \longrightarrow \mathbf{B}\mathbb{G}_{conn}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mo ∇
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,\nabla)
            is a diagram of the form
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi X
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo ⟶
                            %mo ≃
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mi X
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mrow
                            %mpadded{:lspace => "-100%width", :width => "0"}
                              %mo ∇
                          %mo ↘
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mo ⇙
                            %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                              %mo ≃
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mo ↙
                            %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                              %mo ∇
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                            %mi B
                          %msub
                            %mi 𝔾
                            %mi conn
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  X &amp;&amp; \stackrel{\simeq}{\longrightarrow} &amp;&amp;  X
                  \\\
                  \&amp; {}_{\mathllap{\nabla}}\searrow &amp;\swArrow_{\mathrlap{\simeq}}&amp; \swarrow_{\mathrlap{\nabla}}
                  \\\
                  \&amp;&amp; \mathbf{B}\mathbb{G}_{conn}
                  }
                  \\,.
          %p
            This means that for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi H
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{H}
            a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohesive+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-topos"} cohesive (∞,1)-topos
            of geometric spaces of sorts, then the corresponding
            = succeed "-prequantum" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝔾
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{G}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/slice+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-topos"} slice (∞,1)-topos
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi H
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                        %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                          %mi B
                        %msub
                          %mi 𝔾
                          %mi conn
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{H}_{/\mathbf{B}\mathbb{G}_{conn}}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+moduli+stacks"} higher moduli stacks
            for
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+cohomology"} differential cohomology
          %h3#TheGeometricIdeaOfQuantization The geometric idea of quantization
          %p
            Quantization of course was and is motivated by
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/experiment"} experiment
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/observable+universe"} observable universe
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanics"} quantum mechanics
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
            correctly account for experimental observations where
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+mechanics"} classical mechanics
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+field+theory"} classical field theory
            gives no answer or incorrect answers. A historically important example is the phenomenon called the “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ultraviolet+catastrophe"}> ultraviolet catastrophe
            ”, a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/paradox"} paradox
            predicted by classical
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/statistical+mechanics"} statistical mechanics
            which is
            %em not
            observed in nature, and which is corrected by
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanics"} quantum mechanics
          %p
            But one may also ask, independently of experimental input, if there are good formal mathematical reasons and motivations to pass from
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+mechanics"} classical mechanics
            to
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanics"} quantum mechanics
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanics"} quantum mechanics
            by just pondering the mathematical formalism of
            = succeed "?" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+mechanics"} classical mechanics
            = succeed "?)" do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/show/Synthetic+Quantum+Field+Theory"} Synthetic Quantum Field Theory
          %p
            The following spells out an argument to this effect. It will work for readers with a background in modern
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mathematics"} mathematics
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+theory"} Lie theory
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+geometry"} symplectic geometry
          %p
            So to briefly recall, a system of
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+mechanics"} classical mechanics
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prequantum+field+theory"} prequantum mechanics
            is a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/phase+space"} phase space
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+manifold"} symplectic manifold
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ω
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X, \omega)
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+manifold"} Poisson manifold
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebra+of+functions"} algebra of functions
            on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/phase+space"} phase space
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                classical
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/observables"} observables
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+bracket"} Lie bracket
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+bracket"} Poisson bracket
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dynamics"} dynamics
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+mechanics"} classical mechanics
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi H
                  %mo ∈
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mn ∞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H \in C^\infty(X)
            is the function on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/phase+space"} phase space
            which is interpreted as assigning to each configuration of the system its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/energy"} energy
            – the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hamiltonian"} Hamiltonian
            function – then the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+bracket"} Poisson bracket
            with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi H
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H
            yields the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinitesimal+object"} infinitesimal
            time evolution of the system: the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+equation"} differential equation
            famous as
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hamilton%27s+equations"} Hamilton's equations
          %p
            Something to take notice of here is the
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinitesimal+space"} infinitesimal
            nature of the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+bracket"} Poisson bracket
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+algebra"} Lie algebra
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝔤
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{g}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinitesimal+object"} infinitesimal
            approximation to a globally defined object, the corresponding
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+group"} Lie group
            (or generally
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+group"} smooth group
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            is a
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+integration"} Lie integration
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝔤
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{g}
            and that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝔤
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{g}
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+differentiation"} Lie differentiation
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
          %p
            Therefore a natural question to ask is:
            %em
              Since the observables in
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+mechanics"} classical mechanics
              form a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+algebra"} Lie algebra
              under
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+bracket"} Poisson bracket
              = succeed "?" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+group"} Lie group
          %p
            The answer to this is of course “well known” in the literature, in the sense that there are relevant monographs which state the answer. But, maybe surprisingly, the answer to this question is not (at time of this writing) a widely advertized fact that has found its way into the basic educational textbooks. The answer is that this
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+group"} Lie group
            which integrates the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+bracket"} Poisson bracket
            is the “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantomorphism+group"}> quantomorphism group
            ”, an object that seamlessly leads to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanics"} quantum mechanics
            of the system.
          %p
            Before we spell this out in more detail, we need a brief technical aside: of course
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+integration"} Lie integration
            is not quite unique. There may be different global
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+group"} Lie group
            objects with the same
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+algebra"} Lie algebra
          %p
            The simplest example of this is already one of central importance for the issue of quantization, namely, the Lie integration of the abelian
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/line+Lie+algebra"} line Lie algebra
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℝ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+groups"} Lie groups
            associated with it: the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simply+connected+topological+space"} simply connected
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/translation+group"} translation group
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
            itself again, equipped with its canonical additive
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+group"} abelian group
            structure, and the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/discrete+space"} discrete
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quotient"} quotient
            of this by the group of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integers"} integers
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/circle+group"} circle group
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi ℝ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi ℤ
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  U(1) = \mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z}
                  \\,.
          %p
            Notice that it is the discrete and hence “quantized” nature of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integers"} integers
            that makes the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+line"} real line
            become a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/circle"} circle
            here. This is not entirely a coincidence of terminology, but can be traced back to the heart of what is “quantized” about
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanics"} quantum mechanics
          %p
            Namely, one finds that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+bracket"} Poisson bracket
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+algebra"} Lie algebra
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝔭𝔬𝔦𝔰𝔰
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ω
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{poiss}(X,\omega)
            of the classical
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/observables"} observables
            on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/phase+space"} phase space
            is (for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connected+topological+space"} connected
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/manifold"} manifold
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+algebra+extension"} Lie algebra extension
            of the Lie algebra
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝔥𝔞𝔪
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{ham}(X)
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hamiltonian+vector+fields"} Hamiltonian vector fields
            on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            by the
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/line+Lie+algebra"} line Lie algebra
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℝ
                  %mo ⟶
                  %mi 𝔭𝔬𝔦𝔰𝔰
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ω
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⟶
                  %mi 𝔥𝔞𝔪
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\mathbb{R} \longrightarrow \mathfrak{poiss}(X,\omega) \longrightarrow \mathfrak{ham}(X)
                  \\,.
          %p
            This means that under
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+integration"} Lie integration
            the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+bracket"} Poisson bracket
            turns into an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/central+extension"} central extension
            of the group of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hamiltonian+symplectomorphisms"} Hamiltonian symplectomorphisms
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ω
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,\omega)
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℝ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/central+extension"} central extension
            by the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/circle+group"} circle group
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U(1)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+integration"} Lie integration
            to exist,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ω
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,\omega)
            needs to satisfy a quantization condition which says that it admits a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prequantum+line+bundle"} prequantum line bundle
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U(1)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/central+extension"} central extension
            of the group
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Ham
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ω
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ham(X,\omega)
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hamiltonian+symplectomorphisms"} Hamiltonian symplectomorphisms
            exists and is called… the
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantomorphism+group"} quantomorphism group
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi QuantMorph
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ω
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} QuantMorph(X,\omega)
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⟶
                  %mi QuantMorph
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ω
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⟶
                  %mi Ham
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ω
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  U(1) \longrightarrow QuantMorph(X,\omega) \longrightarrow Ham(X,\omega)
                  \\,.
          %p
            While important, for some reason this group is not very well known, which is striking because it contains a small
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subgroup"} subgroup
            which is famous in
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanics"} quantum mechanics
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Heisenberg+group"} Heisenberg group
          %p
            More precisely, whenever
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ω
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,\omega)
            itself has a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hamiltonian+action"} compatible
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group"} group
            structure, notably if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ω
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,\omega)
            is just a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+vector+space"} symplectic vector space
            (regarded as a group under addition of vectors), then we may ask for the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subgroup"} subgroup
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantomorphism+group"} quantomorphism group
            which covers the (left)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action"} action
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/phase+space"} phase space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ω
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,\omega)
            on itself. This is the corresponding
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Heisenberg+group"} Heisenberg group
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Heis
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ω
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Heis(X,\omega)
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U(1)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/central+extension"} central extension
            of the group
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            itself:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⟶
                  %mi Heis
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ω
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⟶
                  %mi X
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  U(1) \longrightarrow Heis(X,\omega) \longrightarrow X
                  \\,.
          %p
            At this point it is worth pausing for a second to note how the hallmark of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanics"} quantum mechanics
            has appeared as if out of nowhere simply by applying
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+integration"} Lie integration
            to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+algebra"} Lie algebraic
            structures in
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+mechanics"} classical mechanics
          %p
            if we think of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+integration"} Lie integrating
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
            to the interesting
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/circle+group"} circle group
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U(1)
            instead of to the uninteresting
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/translation+group"} translation group
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℝ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/basis"} basis
            element
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 1
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1 \in \mathbb{R}
            is canonically “
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i
            ”, the imaginary unit. Therefore one often writes the above
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/central+extension"} central extension
            instead as follows:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi i
                  %mi ℝ
                  %mo ⟶
                  %mi 𝔭𝔬𝔦𝔰𝔰
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ω
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⟶
                  %mi 𝔥𝔞𝔪
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ω
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  i \mathbb{R} \longrightarrow \mathfrak{poiss}(X,\omega) \longrightarrow \mathfrak{ham}(X,\omega)
          %p
            in order to amplify this. But now consider the simple special case where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ω
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mn 2
                  %mo ,
                  %mi d
                  %mi p
                  %mo ∧
                  %mi d
                  %mi q
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,\omega) = (\mathbb{R}^2, d p \wedge d q)
            is the 2-dimensional
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+vector+space"} symplectic vector space
            which is for instance the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/phase+space"} phase space
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/particle"} particle
            propagating on the line. Then a canonical set of generators for the corresponding
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+bracket"} Poisson bracket
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+algebra"} Lie algebra
            consists of the linear functions
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi q
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} q
            of classical mechanics textbook fame, together with the
            %em constant
            function. Under the above Lie theoretic identification, this constant function is the canonical basis element of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi i
                    %mi ℝ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i \mathbb{R}
            = succeed "\u201D." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i
          %p
            With this notation then the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+bracket"} Poisson bracket
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+integration"} Lie integration
            manifest, indeed reads
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi q
                  %mo ,
                  %mi p
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo =
                  %mi i
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  [q,p] = i
                  \\,.
          %p
            Since the choice of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/basis"} basis
            element of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi i
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i \mathbb{R}
            is arbitrary, we may rescale here the
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i
            by any non-vanishing
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+number"} real number
            without changing this statement. If we write “
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℏ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hbar
            ” for this element, then the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+bracket"} Poisson bracket
            instead reads
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi q
                  %mo ,
                  %mi p
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo =
                  %mi i
                  %mi ℏ
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  [q,p] = i \hbar
                  \\,.
          %p
            This is of course the hallmark equation for
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+physics"} quantum physics
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℏ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hbar
            here indeed as
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Planck%27s+constant"} Planck's constant
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+integration"} Lie integration
            of the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+bracket"} Poisson bracket
          %p
            This is only the beginning of the story of quantization, naturally understood and indeed “derived” from applying
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+theory"} Lie theory
            to
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+mechanics"} classical mechanics
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+quantization"} geometric quantization
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantomorphism+group"} quantomorphism group
            which is the non-trivial
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+integration"} Lie integration
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+bracket"} Poisson bracket
            is naturally constructed as follows: given the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+form"} symplectic form
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ω
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/curvature"} curvature
            2-form of a
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U(1)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principal+connection"} principal connection
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∇
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \nabla
            on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+line+bundle"} complex line bundle
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi L
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
            over
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            (this is directly analogous to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dirac+charge+quantization"} Dirac charge quantization
            when instead of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+form"} symplectic form
            on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/phase+space"} phase space
            we consider the the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+strength"} field strength
            2-form of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electromagnetism"} electromagnetism
            on
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi L
                  %mo ,
                  %mo ∇
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (L, \nabla)
            is called a
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prequantum+line+bundle"} prequantum line bundle
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/phase+space"} phase space
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ω
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,\omega)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantomorphism+group"} quantomorphism group
            is simply the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/automorphism+group"} automorphism group
            of the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prequantum+line+bundle"} prequantum line bundle
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diffeomorphisms"} diffeomorphisms
            of the phase space (the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hamiltonian+symplectomorphisms"} Hamiltonian symplectomorphisms
            mentioned above).
          %p
            As such, the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantomorphism+group"} quantomorphism group
            naturally
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action"} acts
            on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space+of+sections"} space of sections
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi L
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/section"} section
            is like a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/wavefunction"} wavefunction
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/phase+space"} phase space
            = succeed "\u201D." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/canonical+coordinates"} canonical coordinates
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/motivic+quantization"} motivic quantization
            for more) it is indeed natural to choose a “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/polarization"}> polarization
            ” of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/phase+space"} phase space
            into
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/canonical+coordinates"} canonical coordinates
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/canonical+momenta"} canonical momenta
            and consider only those
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sections"} sections
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prequantum+line+bundle"} prequantum line bundle
            which depend only on the former. These are the actual
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/wavefunctions"} wavefunctions
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanics"} quantum mechanics
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+states"} quantum states
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subgroup"} subgroup
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantomorphism+group"} quantomorphism group
            which preserves these polarized sections is the group of exponentiated
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+observables"} quantum observables
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ω
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,\omega)
            is the 2-dimensional
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+vector+space"} symplectic vector space
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Heisenberg+group"} Heisenberg group
            with its famous action by multiplication and differentiation operators on the space of complex-valued functions on the real line.
          %h3#InfinitesimalSymmetries Infinitesimal symmetries
          %p
            We discuss here the infinitesimal symmetries of prequantum geometries, exhibited by the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+bracket"} Poisson bracket
            Lie algebra and its higher analogs, the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+bracket+Lie+n-algebra"} Poisson bracket Lie n-algebra
          %h4#PoissonBracketHeisenbergAlgebraInfinitesimalQuantomorphisms Poisson brackets and Heisenberg algebra
          %p
            We discuss the traditional definition of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+bracket"} Poisson bracket
            of a (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presymplectic+manifold"}> pre-
            )
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+manifold"}> symplectic manifold
            , highlighting how conceptually it may be understood as the algebra of infinitesimal symmetries of any of its
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prequantizations"} prequantizations
          #PresymplecticManifold.num_defn
            %h6#definition Definition
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              be a
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+manifold"} smooth manifold
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+2-form"} differential 2-form
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ω
                    %mo ∈
                    %msubsup
                      %mi Ω
                      %mi cl
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega \in \Omega_{cl}^2(X)
              is a
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+form"} symplectic form
              if it is non-degenerate in that the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/kernel"} kernel
              of the operation of contracting with
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+fields"} vector fields
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi ι
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi ω
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi Vect
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⟶
                    %msup
                      %mi Ω
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\iota_{(-)}\omega \colon Vect(X) \longrightarrow \Omega^1(X)
            %p
              is trivial:
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ker
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi ι
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi ω
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ker(\iota_{(-)}\omega) = 0
            %p
              If
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ω
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega
              is just closed with possibly non-trivial kernel, we call it a
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presymplectic+form"} presymplectic form
          #HamiltonianVectorField.num_defn
            %h6#definition_2 Definition
            %p
              Given a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presymplectic+manifold"} presymplectic manifold
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo ,
                      %mi ω
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X, \omega)
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hamiltonian"} Hamiltonian
              for a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+field"} vector field
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi v
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi Vect
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} v \in Vect(X)
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+function"} smooth function
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi H
                    %mo ∈
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mn ∞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H \in C^\infty(X)
              such that
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi ι
                      %mi v
                    %mi ω
                    %mo +
                    %mi d
                    %mi H
                    %mo =
                    %mn 0
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\iota_{v} \omega + d H = 0
                    \\,.
            %p
              If
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi v
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi Vect
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} v \in Vect(X)
              is such that there
              %em exists
              at least one Hamiltonian for it then it is called a
              = succeed "." do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hamiltonian+vector+field"} Hamiltonian vector field
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi HamVect
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ω
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ↪
                    %mi Vect
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    HamVect(X,\omega) \hookrightarrow Vect(X)
            %p
              for the
              = succeed "-" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℝ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+subspace"} linear subspace
              of Hamiltonian vector fields among all
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+fields"} vector fields
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark Remark
            %p
              When
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ω
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega
              is symplectic then, evidently, there is a unique Hamiltonian vector field, def.
              = succeed "," do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#HamiltonianVectorField"}
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prequantum+geometry"} prequantum geometry
              is concerned, this is all that the non-degeneracy condition that makes a closed 2-form be symplectic is for. But we will see that the definitions of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+brackets"} Poisson brackets
              and of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantomorphism+groups"} quantomorphism groups
              directly generalize also to the presymplectic situation, simply by considering not just Hamiltonian fuctions but pairs of a Hamiltonian vector field and a compatible Hamiltonian.
          #PoissonBracket.num_defn
            %h6#definition_3 Definition
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ω
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,\omega)
              be a
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presymplectic+manifold"} presymplectic manifold
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Ham
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ω
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ↪
                    %mi HamVect
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ω
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⊕
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mn ∞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    Ham(X,\omega) \hookrightarrow HamVect(X,\omega) \oplus C^\infty(X)
            %p
              for the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+subspace"} linear subspace
              of the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/direct+sum"} direct sum
              of
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hamiltonian+vector+fields"} Hamiltonian vector fields
              = succeed "," do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#HamiltonianVectorField"}
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+functions"} smooth functions
              on those pairs
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi v
                    %mo ,
                    %mi H
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (v,H)
              for which
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi H
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hamiltonian"} Hamiltonian
              for
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi v
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} v
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Ham
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ω
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≔
                    %mrow
                      %mo {
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi v
                      %mo ,
                      %mi H
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %msub
                        %mi ι
                        %mi v
                      %mi ω
                      %mo +
                      %mi d
                      %mi H
                      %mo =
                      %mn 0
                      %mo }
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    Ham(X,\omega)
                    \\coloneqq
                    \\left\{
                    (v,H) | \iota_v \omega + d H  = 0
                    \\right\}
                    \\,.
            %p
              Define a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bilinear+map"} bilinear map
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mi Ham
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ω
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi Ham
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ω
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⟶
                    %mi Ham
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ω
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    [-,-] \;\colon\;
                    Ham(X,\omega) \otimes Ham(X,\omega)
                    \\longrightarrow
                    Ham(X,\omega)
            %p by
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi v
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi H
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi v
                      %mn 2
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi H
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo ≔
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %msub
                      %mi v
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi v
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi ι
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi v
                          %mn 2
                    %msub
                      %mi ι
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi v
                          %mn 1
                    %mi ω
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo ,
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    [(v_1,H_1), (v_2,H_2)] \coloneqq ([v_1,v_2], \iota_{v_2}\iota_{v_1} \omega)
                    \\,,
            %p
              called the
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+bracket"} Poisson bracket
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %msub
                      %mi v
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi v
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [v_1,v_2]
              is the standard Lie bracket on
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+fields"} vector fields
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝔭𝔬𝔦𝔰𝔰
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ω
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≔
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Ham
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ω
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\mathfrak{poiss}(X,\omega)
                    \\coloneqq
                    (Ham(X,\omega),[-,-])
            %p
              for the resulting
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+algebra"} Lie algebra
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ω
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega
              is symplectic, then
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Ham
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ω
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≃
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mn ∞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ham(X,\omega) \simeq C^\infty(X)
              and hence in this case
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝔭𝔬𝔦𝔰𝔰
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ω
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≃
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mn ∞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\mathfrak{poiss}(X,\omega)
                    \\simeq
                    (C^\infty(X),[-,-])
                    \\,.
          #HeisenbergAlgebraOfSymplecticVectorSpace.num_example
            %h6#example Example
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo =
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mrow
                        %mn 2
                        %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X = \mathbb{R}^{2n}
              and let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ω
                    %mo =
                    %msubsup
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                      %mrow
                        %mi i
                        %mo =
                        %mn 1
                      %mi n
                    %mi d
                    %msub
                      %mi p
                      %mi i
                    %mo ∧
                    %mi d
                    %msup
                      %mi q
                      %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega = \sum_{i = 1}^n d p_i \wedge d q^i
              for
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %msup
                      %mi q
                      %mi i
                    %msubsup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                      %mrow
                        %mi i
                        %mo =
                        %mn 1
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{q^i\}_{i = 1}^n
              the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/canonical+coordinates"} canonical coordinates
              on one copy of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^n
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %msub
                      %mi p
                      %mi i
                    %msubsup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                      %mrow
                        %mi i
                        %mo =
                        %mn 1
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{p_i\}_{i = 1}^n
              that on the other (“
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/canonical+momenta"}> canonical momenta
              ”). Hence let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ω
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,\omega)
              be a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+vector+space"} symplectic vector space
              of dimension
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 2
                      %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2n
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+manifold"} symplectic manifold
            %p
              Then
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Vect
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Vect(X)
              is
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+span"} spanned
              over
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mn ∞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^\infty(X)
              by the canonical bases vector fields
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %msub
                        %mo ∂
                        %mrow
                          %msup
                            %mi q
                            %mi i
                      %mo ,
                      %msub
                        %mo ∂
                        %mrow
                          %msup
                            %mi p
                            %mi i
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{\partial_{q^i}, \partial_{p^i}\}
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hamiltonian+vector+fields"} Hamiltonian vector fields
              via
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi ι
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mo ∂
                          %mrow
                            %msup
                              %mi q
                              %mi i
                    %mi ω
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mi d
                    %msub
                      %mi p
                      %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\iota_{\partial_{q^i}} \omega = - d p_i
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi ι
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mo ∂
                          %mrow
                            %msub
                              %mi p
                              %mi i
                    %mi ω
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} +
                    %mi d
                    %msup
                      %mi q
                      %mi i
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\iota_{\partial_{p_i}} \omega = + d q^i
                    \\,.
            %p
              Moreover, since
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              is
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connected+topological+space"} connected
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi i
                    %mo ∈
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mn ∞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{i} \in C^\infty(X)
              for the unit constant function, then the nontrivial Poisson brackets between the basis vector fields are
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %msup
                      %mi q
                      %mi i
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi p
                      %mi j
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo ≔
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %msub
                      %mo ∂
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi p
                          %mi i
                    %mo ,
                    %msup
                      %mi q
                      %mi i
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mo ∂
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi q
                          %mi j
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi p
                      %mi j
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %msubsup
                      %mi δ
                      %mi j
                      %mi i
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi i
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %msubsup
                      %mi δ
                      %mi j
                      %mi i
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi i
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    [q^i, p_j]
                    \\coloneqq
                    [(-\partial_{p_i}, q^i), (\partial_{q^j}, p_j)]
                    \=
                    \-
                    \\delta_j^i
                    (0, \mathbf{i})
                    \=
                    \-
                    \\delta_j^i
                    \\mathbf{i}
                    \\,.
            %p
              This is called the
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Heisenberg+algebra"} Heisenberg algebra
            %p
              More generally, the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hamiltonian+vector+fields"} Hamiltonian vector fields
              corresponding to
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quadratic+Hamiltonians"} quadratic Hamiltonians
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/polynomials"} polynomials
              in the
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %msup
                      %mi q
                      %mi i
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{q^i\}
              and
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %msub
                        %mi p
                        %mi i
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{p_i\}
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/affine+symplectic+group"} affine symplectic group
              of
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo ,
                      %mi ω
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,\omega)
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extended+affine+symplectic+group"} extended affine symplectic group
          %h4#infinitesimal_quantomorphisms Infinitesimal quantomorphisms
          %p
            Example
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#HeisenbergAlgebraOfSymplecticVectorSpace"}
            serves to motivate a more conceptual origin of the definition of the Poisson bracket in def.
            = succeed "." do
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#PoissonBracket"}
          .num_example
            %h6#example_2 Example
            %p Write
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi θ
                    %mo ≔
                    %munderover
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                      %mrow
                        %mi i
                        %mo =
                        %mn 1
                      %mi n
                    %msub
                      %mi p
                      %mi i
                    %mi d
                    %msub
                      %mi q
                      %mi i
                    %mo ∈
                    %msup
                      %mi Ω
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mrow
                        %mn 2
                        %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\theta \coloneqq \sum_{i = 1}^n p_i d q_i \in \Omega^1(\mathbb{R}^{2n})
            %p
              for the canonical choice of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+1-form"} differential 1-form
              satisfying
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi d
                    %mi θ
                    %mo =
                    %mi ω
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    d \theta = \omega
                    \\,.
            %p
              If we regard
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mrow
                        %mn 2
                        %mi n
                    %mo ≃
                    %msup
                      %mi T
                      %mo *
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^{2n} \simeq T^\ast \mathbb{R}^n
              as the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cotangent+bundle"} cotangent bundle
              of the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartesian+space"} Cartesian space
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi ℝ
                        %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^n
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Liouville-Poincar%C3%A9+1-form"} Liouville-Poincaré 1-form
            %p
              Since
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mrow
                        %mn 2
                        %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^{2n}
              is
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/contractible+topological+space"} contractible
              as a
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/circle+bundle"} circle bundle
              over it is necessarily trivial, and hence any choice of 1-form such as
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi θ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \theta
              may canonically be thought of as being a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connection+on+a+bundle"} connection
              on the trivial
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principal+U%281%29-bundle"} principal U(1)-bundle
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi θ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \theta
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prequantization"} prequantization
              of
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msup
                        %mi ℝ
                        %mrow
                          %mn 2
                          %mi n
                      %mo ,
                      %msubsup
                        %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                        %mrow
                          %mi i
                          %mo =
                          %mn 1
                        %mi n
                      %mi d
                      %msub
                        %mi p
                        %mi i
                      %mo ∧
                      %mi d
                      %msup
                        %mi q
                        %mi i
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\mathbb{R}^{2n}, \sum_{i=1}^n d p_i \wedge d q^i)
            %p
              Being thus a
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/circle+bundle+with+connection"} circle bundle with connection
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi θ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \theta
              has more symmetry than its
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/curvature"} curvature
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ω
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega
              has: for
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi α
                    %mo ∈
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mn ∞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mrow
                        %mn 2
                        %mi n
                    %mo ,
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha \in C^\infty(\mathbb{R}^{2n}, U(1))
              any smooth function, then
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi θ
                    %mo ↦
                    %mi θ
                    %mo +
                    %mi d
                    %mi α
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\theta \mapsto \theta + d\alpha
            %p
              is the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+transformation"} gauge transformation
              of
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi θ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \theta
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prequantization"} prequantization
              of
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ω
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega
            %p
              Hence while a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+field"} vector field
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi v
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} v
              is said to preserve
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ω
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega
              (is a
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+vector+field"} symplectic vector field
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+derivative"} Lie derivative
              of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ω
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega
              along
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi v
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} v
              vanishes
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi ℒ
                      %mi v
                    %mi ω
                    %mo =
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\mathcal{L}_v \omega = 0
            %p
              in the presence of a choice for
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi θ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \theta
              the right condition to ask for is that there is
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi α
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha
              such that
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi ℒ
                      %mi v
                    %mi θ
                    %mo =
                    %mi d
                    %mi α
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\mathcal{L}_v \theta = d \alpha
                    \\,.
          %p
            For more on this see also at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prequantized+Lagrangian+correspondence"} prequantized Lagrangian correspondence
          %p Notice then the following basic but important fact.
          .num_prop
            %h6#proposition Proposition
            %p
              For
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ω
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,\omega)
              a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presymplectic+manifold"} presymplectic manifold
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi θ
                    %mo ∈
                    %msup
                      %mi Ω
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \theta \in \Omega^1(X)
              a 1-form such that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi d
                    %mi θ
                    %mo =
                    %mi ω
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d \theta = \omega
              then for
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi v
                    %mo ,
                    %mi α
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi Vect
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⊕
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mn ∞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (v,\alpha) \in Vect(X)\oplus C^\infty(X)
              the condition
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi ℒ
                      %mi v
                    %mi θ
                    %mo =
                    %mi d
                    %mi α
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{L}_v \theta = d \alpha
              is equivalent to the condition that makes
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi H
                    %mo ≔
                    %msub
                      %mi ι
                      %mi v
                    %mi θ
                    %mo −
                    %mi α
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    H \coloneqq \iota_v \theta - \alpha
            %p
              a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hamiltonian"} Hamiltonian
              for
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi v
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} v
              according to def.
              = succeed ":" do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#HamiltonianVectorField"}
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi ℒ
                      %mi v
                    %mi θ
                    %mo =
                    %mi d
                    %mi α
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo ⇔
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %msub
                      %mi ι
                      %mi v
                    %mi ω
                    %mo +
                    %mi d
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %munder
                      %munder
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi ι
                            %mi v
                          %mi θ
                          %mo −
                          %mi α
                        %mo ⏟
                      %mi H
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mn 0
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\mathcal{L}_v \theta = d \alpha
                    \\;\;\;\Leftrightarrow\;\;\;
                    \\iota_v \omega + d (\underset{H}{\underbrace{\iota_v \theta - \alpha}}) = 0
                    \\,.
            %p
              Moreover, the
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+bracket"} Poisson bracket
              = succeed "," do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#PoissonBracket"}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi v
                      %mi i
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi α
                      %mi i
                    %mo −
                    %msub
                      %mi ι
                      %mi v
                    %mi θ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (v_i, \alpha_i -\iota_v \theta)
              is equivalently given by the skew-symmetric
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+derivative"} Lie derivative
              of the corresponding vector fields on the
              = succeed ":" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi α
                        %mi i
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha_i
            #eq:EquationForLieHomomorphism.maruku-equation
              %span.maruku-eq-number (1)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi ι
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                        %msub
                          %mi v
                          %mn 1
                        %mo ,
                        %msub
                          %mi v
                          %mn 2
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mi θ
                    %mo −
                    %msub
                      %mi ι
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi v
                          %mn 2
                    %msub
                      %mi ι
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi v
                          %mn 1
                    %mi ω
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mi ℒ
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi v
                          %mn 1
                    %msub
                      %mi α
                      %mn 2
                    %mo −
                    %msub
                      %mi ℒ
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi v
                          %mn 2
                    %msub
                      %mi α
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\iota_{[v_1,v_2]} \theta
                    \-
                    \\iota_{v_2}\iota_{v_1}\omega
                    \=
                    \\mathcal{L}_{v_1} \alpha_2 - \mathcal{L}_{v_2} \alpha_1
          .proof
            %h6#proof Proof
            %p
              Using
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartan%27s+magic+formula"} Cartan's magic formula
              and by the prequantization condition
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi d
                    %mi θ
                    %mo =
                    %mi ω
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d \theta = \omega
              the we have
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable{:columnalign => "right left right left right left right left right left", :columnspacing => "0em", :displaystyle => "true"}
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %msub
                              %mi ℒ
                              %mi v
                            %mi θ
                          %mtd
                            %mo =
                            %msub
                              %mi ι
                              %mi v
                            %mi d
                            %mi θ
                            %mo +
                            %mi d
